---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Braided challah | Recipes">
  <article class="recipe">
    <header class="recipe-header">
      <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/recipes">← All recipes</a></p>
      <h1>Braided challah</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A reliable enriched loaf in the spirit of J. Kenji López-Alt&apos;s
        approach to bread: bread flour for chew, full kneading to develop
        gluten, a long bulk fermentation, and a patient final proof. The
        result is soft and feathery on the inside with a deeply burnished,
        glossy crust. Day-old slices are exactly what the French toast
        recipe wants.
      </p>
      <ul class="recipe-meta">
        <li><strong>Yield</strong> · 1 large loaf</li>
        <li><strong>Active</strong> · 30 minutes</li>
        <li><strong>Total</strong> · ~4 hours</li>
      </ul>
    </header>

    <section class="recipe-section">
      <h2>Ingredients</h2>
      <ul class="ingredients">
        <li>4 cups (480 g) bread flour, plus more for dusting</li>
        <li>¼ cup (50 g) granulated sugar</li>
        <li>2¼ teaspoons (7 g / 1 packet) instant yeast</li>
        <li>1½ teaspoons (6 g) kosher salt</li>
        <li>3 large eggs (2 for the dough, 1 for the wash)</li>
        <li>½ cup (120 g) warm water, about 105°F</li>
        <li>¼ cup (50 g) neutral oil, plus more for the bowl</li>
        <li>2 tablespoons (40 g) honey</li>
        <li>1 tablespoon water, for the egg wash</li>
        <li>Sesame or poppy seeds, optional</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="recipe-section">
      <h2>Method</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          In the bowl of a stand mixer fitted with the dough hook, whisk
          together the flour, sugar, yeast, and salt.
        </li>
        <li>
          In a separate bowl, whisk 2 eggs with the warm water, oil, and
          honey until smooth.
        </li>
        <li>
          Pour the wet ingredients into the dry. Mix on low until the dough
          comes together as a shaggy mass, about 1 minute. Increase the
          speed to medium and knead for 6–8 minutes, until the dough is
          smooth, elastic, and pulls cleanly from the sides of the bowl.
          It should feel soft but not tacky; add a tablespoon more flour
          if it sticks.
        </li>
        <li>
          Transfer to a lightly oiled bowl, turn to coat, cover, and let
          rise at warm room temperature until doubled in size, 1½–2 hours.
        </li>
        <li>
          Turn the dough out onto a lightly floured counter. Divide into 4
          equal pieces (or 6 for a more elaborate braid). Roll each piece
          into a strand 16–18 inches long, slightly thicker in the middle
          and tapered at the ends.
        </li>
        <li>
          Line up the strands and pinch them together at the top. For a
          simple four-strand braid: take the rightmost strand over 2, then
          the leftmost strand over 2, and repeat until done. Pinch the
          ends together and tuck under the loaf.
        </li>
        <li>
          Transfer to a parchment-lined sheet pan. Cover loosely with
          plastic or a clean towel and proof until visibly puffy and a
          finger pressed gently into the dough leaves a slow-rebounding
          dent, 45–60 minutes.
        </li>
        <li>
          Heat the oven to 350°F (175°C) during the last 30 minutes of
          proofing.
        </li>
        <li>
          Beat the remaining egg with 1 tablespoon water and a small pinch
          of salt. Brush evenly over the top and sides of the loaf, paying
          attention to the crevices of the braid. Sprinkle with seeds if
          using.
        </li>
        <li>
          Bake until deeply burnished and the internal temperature reads
          190–195°F at the center, 28–32 minutes. Rotate the pan halfway
          through. If the top is browning faster than the inside is
          cooking, tent loosely with foil for the last few minutes.
        </li>
        <li>
          Cool on a wire rack at least 30 minutes before slicing — the
          crumb is still setting up.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="recipe-section">
      <h2>Notes</h2>
      <ul class="notes">
        <li>
          Bread flour matters here. The higher protein gives the crumb a
          tender chew and helps the strands hold their shape through the
          final proof and bake.
        </li>
        <li>
          If the kitchen is cold, proof in the oven with just the light on,
          or in an off oven next to a mug of just-boiled water.
        </li>
        <li>
          Leftovers go stale quickly — which is perfect, because day-old
          slices are exactly what the <a href="/recipes/french-toast">French
          toast recipe</a> calls for.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
